package com.hpcai270

// Days 220–236: AI data pipelines, GPU data movement, checkpointing and storage architecture.
val hpcDaysPart19 = listOf(
Day(220,"AI training data lifecycle","Raw data to training-ready data","AI infrastructure begins with data. Understand ingest, validation, preprocessing, sharding, storage, caching, training, checkpointing and archive.","FLOW\n\nOBJECT/INGEST → CLEAN → TRANSFORM → SHARD → CURATE → FAST SHARED STORAGE → GPU TRAINING → CHECKPOINT → MODEL ARTIFACT → ARCHIVE.","STORAGE\n\nDifferent stages need different semantics and performance. Object storage is excellent for durable datasets; parallel filesystems and local NVMe can serve active training.","LAB\n\nDesign the data lifecycle for a 1 PB dataset. Identify which copies are durable, temporary and reconstructible.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy should one storage system not necessarily serve every stage? Cost, semantics, latency, bandwidth and lifecycle differ.","GOAL\n\nDesign data movement before buying storage."),
Day(221,"Dataset formats","Why file format affects infrastructure","AI frameworks commonly use formats that change access pattern, compression, metadata and parallelism. Storage architecture must understand application format.","EXAMPLES\n\nParquet for analytics, WebDataset/tar shards, TFRecord, HDF5, Arrow and framework-specific formats. Exact choice depends on workload.","PATTERN\n\nMillions of tiny files can create metadata storms; large packed shards can improve sequential access but may reduce random sample flexibility.","LAB\n\nDesign a dataset as 10 million files versus 10,000 shards. Estimate metadata operation differences conceptually.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can changing dataset format improve training performance without changing storage hardware? It changes I/O locality and request overhead.","GOAL\n\nTreat data format as part of storage design."),
Day(222,"Data preprocessing pipelines","CPU, storage and network overlap","Preprocessing can become the bottleneck before training starts. It includes decode, tokenize, augment, normalize and shard operations.","PIPELINE\n\nObject store → CPU workers → cache/local NVMe → shared storage → GPU. Parallelism must be controlled to avoid overwhelming storage.","METRICS\n\nSamples/sec, CPU utilization, cache hit rate, storage read bandwidth, metadata rate and queue depth.","LAB\n\nDesign a preprocessing benchmark and identify whether adding CPU workers would improve throughput or simply create storage contention.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can more DataLoader workers make training slower? They can increase contention, context switching and storage metadata/concurrency pressure.","GOAL\n\nTune the complete input pipeline."),
Day(223,"GPU memory and data movement","HBM, system RAM and storage","GPU HBM is extremely fast but limited. Data often moves storage → system RAM → GPU memory, with optional direct paths.","PATH\n\nStorage → NIC/PCIe → host memory → GPU memory. Optimized systems can reduce copies or CPU involvement.","AI\n\nBatch size, prefetching, pinned memory and asynchronous transfers determine whether GPU computation overlaps data movement.","LAB\n\nDraw the memory hierarchy and mark approximate relative goals: capacity increases as speed decreases from HBM to system RAM to NVMe to shared storage to object/archive.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy not keep the entire dataset in GPU memory? Cost and capacity. Infrastructure uses multiple tiers and moves the active working set.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand AI as a data-movement problem."),
Day(224,"GPUDirect Storage","GPU-to-storage data path","GPUDirect Storage enables supported systems to move data between storage and GPU memory with reduced CPU involvement, depending on hardware/software path.","CONCEPT\n\nTraditional path often involves storage → CPU memory → GPU. GDS can optimize supported paths toward storage → GPU memory.","REQUIREMENTS\n\nCompatible GPU, storage, NIC/PCIe topology, filesystem/storage stack and supported software versions. Compatibility must be validated for the exact environment.","PERFORMANCE\n\nMeasure end-to-end application impact. Lower CPU overhead or higher transfer throughput does not automatically guarantee faster training.","LAB\n\nDraw traditional versus GDS paths. Identify where CPU copies are reduced and where PCIe topology still matters.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is GDS solving? Data movement overhead between storage and GPU, not the fundamental need for good storage/network architecture.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand the data path, not just the acronym."),
Day(225,"NVLink and GPU communication","GPU-to-GPU is not storage I/O","NVLink provides high-bandwidth GPU interconnect within supported systems. It solves a different problem from storage networking.","SEPARATE PATHS\n\nGPU ↔ GPU: NVLink/NVSwitch where supported.\nGPU ↔ storage: PCIe/NIC/fabric/storage.\nGPU ↔ GPU across nodes: network fabric, commonly InfiniBand/RoCE.","AI\n\nNCCL uses optimized communication paths for collectives. Storage performance cannot be inferred from NVLink performance.","LAB\n\nDraw a multi-GPU node and mark NVLink, PCIe, NIC and storage paths with different arrows.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is NVLink not a replacement for InfiniBand? They connect different scopes and serve different communication paths.","GOAL\n\nKeep GPU fabric and storage fabric concepts distinct."),
Day(226,"NCCL and collectives","Why one slow rank can hold everyone","Distributed training uses collectives such as all-reduce. Workers synchronize, so stragglers can reduce effective GPU utilization.","MODEL\n\nRank 0…N → collective → synchronization. A slow rank or network path can delay the group.","STORAGE CONNECTION\n\nInput stalls can desynchronize workers; checkpoint operations can synchronize writes. Storage and network bottlenecks can therefore appear as GPU idle time.","LAB\n\nCreate a timeline where seven ranks finish input quickly and one rank is delayed by storage. Explain why the other ranks may wait.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy does tail latency matter more in synchronized AI than in an independent request workload? One slow participant can hold the group.","GOAL\n\nUnderstand distributed training synchronization."),
Day(227,"Checkpoint architecture","Local staging to durable storage","Checkpointing should be designed as a pipeline rather than a single synchronous write.","PATTERN\n\nGPU state → local memory/NVMe staging → parallel durable write → metadata/catalog → object/archive copy where appropriate.","TRADE-OFFS\n\nLocal staging reduces training interruption but adds failure/recovery considerations. Durable storage provides protection but may be slower.","SIZING\n\nCheckpoint size × frequency determines sustained write bandwidth and capacity growth. Burst bandwidth can be much higher than average.","LAB\n\nFor 512 GPUs and a 8 TB checkpoint every 20 minutes, calculate average bandwidth requirement and discuss burst/headroom.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you keep training running while checkpoint persistence completes? Discuss asynchronous checkpointing and failure semantics.","GOAL\n\nDesign for both performance and recoverability."),
Day(228,"Model artifacts","Models are data too","Final models, tokenizer files, configurations, evaluation results and provenance need lifecycle management and reproducibility.","ARTIFACT GRAPH\n\nDataset version → code version → container digest → training config → checkpoint → evaluation → model artifact.","STORAGE\n\nObject storage is often appropriate for immutable artifacts; metadata systems/catalogs connect artifacts to experiments.","LAB\n\nDesign an artifact naming/versioning scheme that allows an engineer to reproduce a model six months later.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is a model file alone insufficient for reproducibility? Dependencies, data version, code, configuration and environment matter.","GOAL\n\nTreat models as governed infrastructure artifacts."),
Day(229,"Data locality","Move compute or move data","Distributed systems trade compute placement against data movement. Moving terabytes across a fabric can be more expensive than scheduling work near data.","DECISION\n\nIf data is hot and shared, schedule compute near the storage path. If compute is elastic and data is small, moving data may be simpler.","CLOUD\n\nCloud regions, availability zones and egress charges add another dimension to locality.","LAB\n\nCompare moving 100 TB to a GPU cluster versus moving the cluster workload to where the data already lives. Include network and time.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is data gravity real? Large datasets are expensive to move in bandwidth, time, operational risk and sometimes money.","GOAL\n\nInclude data movement in architecture decisions."),
Day(230,"Storage tiers for AI","Local NVMe, parallel storage, object and archive","A strong AI platform uses tiers because no single medium optimizes latency, bandwidth, capacity and cost simultaneously.","TIERS\n\nGPU-local NVMe → node cache → high-performance shared filesystem → object storage → archive/cold tier.","PLACEMENT\n\nActive working set near compute; durable source data in object/parallel storage; completed artifacts in lower-cost tiers.","LAB\n\nDesign lifecycle rules for datasets, checkpoints and logs. Include expected access frequency.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can local NVMe improve GPU utilization even if shared storage is faster in aggregate? It can reduce repeated network access and provide low-latency caching.","GOAL\n\nDesign data hierarchy, not a single storage box."),
Day(231,"AI storage capacity planning","Capacity plus bandwidth plus bursts","AI storage sizing must include dataset growth, checkpoints, replicas, scratch, cache and operational headroom.","FORM\n\nRequired capacity = active data + versions + checkpoints + metadata/overhead + protection + growth + operational headroom.","BANDWIDTH\n\nAggregate bandwidth = clients × average demand, bounded by application behavior and fabric/storage limits. Use measured workload where possible.","LAB\n\nCreate a 2 PB logical dataset plan with 20% annual growth and weekly checkpoints. Define usable and raw capacity assumptions.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy can a 2 PB system need significantly more than 2 PB raw? Protection, filesystem/object overhead and free-space requirements.","GOAL\n\nSize the service, not just the disks."),
Day(232,"AI storage performance SLOs","Define what ‘fast enough’ means","An SLO should connect infrastructure performance to application outcomes.","EXAMPLES\n\nDataset startup under 2 minutes; sustained training read bandwidth ≥X; checkpoint completion under Y minutes; p99 metadata latency under Z; recovery throughput above a defined floor.","WHY\n\nVendor specifications are not user-facing objectives. SLOs let operations know when the platform is healthy enough for workloads.","LAB\n\nWrite five SLOs for a 256-GPU training service and define measurement sources.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow would you choose an SLO? Start from application requirements, error budget/risk and measured baseline—not from hardware marketing.","GOAL\n\nConnect infrastructure metrics to business/workload outcomes."),
Day(233,"AI storage failure scenarios","What breaks in the real world","Practice failures: one storage node, one fabric link, one metadata service, one GPU node, one object gateway and one entire rack.","METHOD\n\nFor each failure: blast radius → surviving path → degraded performance → recovery mechanism → user communication → validation.","DESIGN\n\nA resilient platform should fail predictably. Redundancy that cannot be tested is an assumption.","LAB\n\nCreate six failure cards and answer each without looking at notes.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhat is your first question during a storage outage? ‘What changed and what is the blast radius?’ then gather evidence.","GOAL\n\nPractice calm, structured incident reasoning."),
Day(234,"AI data security","Dataset access and model protection","AI platforms contain sensitive training data, credentials and proprietary models. Security must cover data, identities, containers, APIs and management planes.","LAYERS\n\nIdentity → authorization → network isolation → encryption → secrets → audit → artifact integrity.","STORAGE\n\nSeparate user datasets, shared project data and platform metadata. Use immutable or controlled artifact storage where appropriate.","LAB\n\nDesign access for three teams and a model-serving service account. Specify what each can read/write.","INTERVIEW\n\nHow do you protect a model artifact from unauthorized replacement? Include identity, write controls, integrity verification and audit.","GOAL\n\nTreat AI data as enterprise-critical information."),
Day(235,"AI data governance","Lineage, retention and reproducibility","Governance answers where data came from, which version was used, who changed it and how long it should remain.","LINEAGE\n\nSource → dataset version → preprocessing → training run → checkpoint → model → deployment.","STORAGE\n\nObject versioning, immutable artifacts, metadata catalogs and lifecycle policies can support lineage.","LAB\n\nCreate a minimal metadata schema for datasets and models: owner, version, checksum, source, retention, classification and dependencies.","INTERVIEW\n\nWhy is governance relevant to infrastructure engineers? Because storage lifecycle and access policy implement the governance contract.","GOAL\n\nConnect storage administration to AI reproducibility."),
Day(236,"AI storage checkpoint","Design the complete data plane","You should now be able to design data movement from object ingest to GPU training and durable checkpoint/archive.","WHITEBOARD\n\nOBJECT → PREPROCESS → CACHE → PARALLEL STORAGE → RDMA FABRIC → GPU → CHECKPOINT → OBJECT/ARCHIVE.","QUESTIONS\n\nWhere is the hot set? What is the bandwidth target? How are small files handled? How are checkpoints protected? What happens during a storage failure?",
"LAB\n\nDesign for 512 GPUs, 10 PB source data, 20 TB checkpoints and 99.9% training-service availability. State assumptions and test plan.","INTERVIEW\n\nExplain why your storage design maximizes GPU productivity without overbuilding every tier.","STANDARD\n\nYou can now discuss AI storage as a lifecycle and data-movement system.")
)
